The bar in the look of tmux's status line (configs/tmux/tmux.conf): blocks
joined by tmux's powerline arrows, U+E0B0 and U+E0B2, which dwm draws as
triangles, antialiased through XRender (drw_arrow), as wide at the blocks'
height as the font's glyphs are in a terminal's cell. On the left the
layout symbol takes the place of tmux's session name, in SchemeLayout,
then tmux's grey step (SchemeChip, two spaces wide), and the tags follow as
tmux's windows: the selected one black on its own colour (tagcolours)
between arrows, as tmux's current window is on the accent, the others in
their colour or dimmed as before, each as wide as it would be selected.
This replaces chadwmbar.diff's underline under the selected tag and moves
the layout symbol in front of the tags, where buttonpress now looks for
it. The focused window's chips are joined by arrows as chevron's prompt is
(drawchipline), and in another window an agent waiting on you is its icon
between arrows. In the status text, ^<#rrggbb^ sets the background as ^b^
does, through an arrow: the new colour pointing left over the old, as
tmux's U+E0B2 in #[fg=new,bg=old]; ^>#rrggbb^ points right, the old over
the new. Every block stops statusvpad short of the bar's top and bottom
(barstyle.diff). Applies after statusclick.diff.

